<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.4.3: Exponential Change: The Growth Factor</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01301</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.4.3: Exponential Change: The Growth Factor</md:title>
    <md:uuid>49c80861-9bdd-42d6-aafc-1ec7907a405a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001">
<para id="para-00001">In an exponential function, the output is multiplied by the same factor every time the input increases by one. The multiplier is called the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">growth factor</term>. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
  <item> In a biology lab, 500 bacteria reproduce by splitting. Every hour, on the hour, each bacterium splits into two bacteria. Fill in the table by writing an expression to show the number of bacteria after each hour.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002"><title>Activity</title><exercise id="45f1e65a-e06a-4a68-96c1-c4915b0f12af" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00001"><problem id="prob-00001"><table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>hour</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>number of bacteria</emphasis></entry></row>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry> 0 </entry>
            <entry> 500 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 1 </entry>
            <entry>a. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 2 </entry>
            <entry>b. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 3 </entry>
            <entry>c. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 6 </entry>
            <entry>d. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> \(t\) </entry>
            <entry>e. _____</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      
      <para id="para-00002">_____ </para>
        <para id="para-00003">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00004">\(500+2\)</para><para id="para-00005">\(500⋅2\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00006">Correct!</para><para id="para-00007">Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003"><exercise id="09d44157-dda4-4f73-a594-d4763128ee00" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00002"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00008"> _____</para>
        <para id="para-00009">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00010">\(500+2+2\)</para><para id="para-00011">\(500⋅2⋅2\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00012">Correct!</para><para id="para-00013">Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00004"><exercise id="ccb60b8d-2271-47f4-a71e-5bddc5341d0c" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00003"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00014">_____</para>
        <para id="para-00015">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00016">\(500+2+2+2\)</para><para id="para-00017">\(500⋅2⋅2⋅2\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00018">Correct! The answer could also be written \(500⋅2^3\).</para><para id="para-00019">Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00005"><exercise id="f695ce12-a152-4149-a08d-87794c510aa2" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00004"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00020">_____</para>
        <para id="para-00021">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00022">\(500+2+2+2+2\)</para><para id="para-00023">\(500+2+2+2+2+2+2\)</para><para id="para-00024">\(500⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2\)</para><para id="para-00025">\(500⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00026">Correct! The answer could also be written \(500\cdot2^6\).</para><para id="para-00027">Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00006"><exercise id="f73d18f8-613f-407f-ad39-e58f0fb533de" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00005"><problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00028">_____</para>
        <para id="para-00029">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00030">\(500+2t\)</para><para id="para-00031">\(500+2^t\)</para><para id="para-00032">\(500⋅2^t\)</para><para id="para-00033">\(500⋅2⋅t\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00034">Correct!</para><para id="para-00035">Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00007"><exercise id="2857bb7f-d0f9-4dbd-a6cb-ef80fde33db9" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00006"><problem id="prob-00006">
      <para id="para-00036">Which equation relates \(n\), the number of bacteria, to \(t\), the number of hours?</para>
        <para id="para-00037">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00038">\(n=500+2t\)</para><para id="para-00039">\(n=500+2^t\)</para><para id="para-00040">\(n=500⋅2^t\)</para><para id="para-00041">\(n=500⋅2⋅t\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00006"><para id="para-00042">Correct!</para><para id="para-00043">Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00007">
  <problem id="prob-00007">
    <para id="para-00044">Use your equation to find \(n\) when \(t\) is 0. What does this value of \(n\) mean in this situation?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00007">
    
    <para id="para-00045">Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00046">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00047"> 500; When \(t\) is 0, \(n=500\cdot2^0\). Because \(2^0=1\), \(n=500\cdot1\) or \(n=500\). 500 is the number of bacteria at the starting time or at hour 0.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00008">
  <problem id="prob-00008">
    <para id="para-00048"> In a different biology lab, a population of single-cell parasites also reproduces hourly. An equation that gives the number of parasites, \(p\), after \(t\) hours is \(p=100\cdot3^t\). Explain what the numbers 100 and 3 mean in this situation. </para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00008">
    
    <para id="para-00049">Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00050">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00051"> 100 is the number of parasites at hour 0 because \(100\cdot3^0=100\). The number 3 means that every hour, the number of parasites triples.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
